    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Juche holds that the masses of the people are the masters of the revolution and that a country prospers once it achieves political, economic and military self-reliance, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>'s government pursued from the 1950s.</li><li>In the famine of 1994 to 1999 estimates of the dead range from 240,000 to 3,500,000, and the UN commission of inquiry of 2014 found violations by the state, including prison camps and executions, that the government rejects.</li><li>Scholars disagree about what Juche is: Brian Reynolds Myers likens it to Japanese fascism and ultranationalism, Charles K. Armstrong calls it a quasi-religion, and Derek R. Ford stresses its continuity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Juche was a word of German philosophy before it was a doctrine: it was coined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1887 to translate Subjekt, and North Korean editions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> used it even before 1955. The Juche calendar counts years from 1912, the year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a>'s birth.</p></aside>
    </section>

